d herself also recorded a large number of her poems while she remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village.\" This is a claimed report by Kaul. It states Arinimaal remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village. Does this advance husband? It says beloved, not husband. Not \"husband who was also poet\". Could be a fact about separation, not directly asked. Maybe not.\n\nAnother: \"Shashi Shekhar Toshakhani... clarifies further. He writes: 'This is one of the most popular Kashmiri songs... Mehmud Gaami ... has also used its few lines... some critics like Ghulam Nabi Khayal consider it to be originally of Mehmud Gaami. But by detailed analysis and comparison, Autar Krishen Rehbar has proved that the basic lines are that of Arinimaal only. Rehbar has clearly shown that these are two different poems, only its refrain... common in both.'\" This supports Arinimaal authorship of basic lines, not OneGap.\n\nAnother: \"Kamil ... regarding 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' poem, he says... Azad ... assumes the poem to be that of Munshi Bhawani Das. In another twist, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in the basket of Habba Khatoon. He says, 'This poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon. And genuinely so, because she has, during her royal times, composed some poems with twisted words...'\" This mentions Habba Khatoon. OneGap asks whether Arinimaal lived centuries after a renowned poetess known by a name derived from her immense beauty. If that poetess is Habba Khatoon, then observation mentions Habba Khatoon and Arinimaal in connection with same poem, but not temporal relation \"centuries after\". It says Kamil attributes a poem to Habba Khatoon; not Arinimaal lived after. Could we extract \"Amin Kamil attributed the poem 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' to Habba Khatoon, saying she composed poems with twisted words during her royal times.\" This is about Habba Khatoon but not Arinimaal's relation. Does it advance OneGap? Maybe not.\n\nCould there be any stateme